<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amorbach</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amorbach is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odenwald">Odenwald</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-franconia">Lower Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. It grew around a Benedictine abbey founded in the 8th century, which later belonged to the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. In 1803 the secularised abbey and its lands were given to the princes of Leiningen as compensation for their losses west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and the former abbey remains the family's residence; its baroque church is famous for its large organ.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amorbach.jpg" alt="Amorbach" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jensre · CC BY-SA 1.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amorbach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
